WEBVTT

00:01.056 --> 00:04.235
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:04.335 --> 00:07.604
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:07.638 --> 00:11.174
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.208 --> 00:14.177
♪ On which we used to rely?

00:14.211 --> 00:17.680
♪ Lucky there's a family guy

00:17.715 --> 00:20.984
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.018 --> 00:22.552
♪ All the things that make us

00:22.586 --> 00:23.920
♪ Laugh and cry

00:23.954 --> 00:26.162
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:35.165 --> 00:36.499
Good afternoon, Quahog.

00:36.533 --> 00:38.468
Our top story:
price wars are heating up

00:38.502 --> 00:40.236
between all of
the major airlines

00:40.271 --> 00:42.405
and even a couple of
the little crashy ones.

00:42.439 --> 00:44.173
We now go live
to Tricia Takanawa

00:44.208 --> 00:45.541
with more on the story.

00:45.576 --> 00:49.112
Tom, I'm standing here at
Boston's Logan Airport,

00:49.146 --> 00:51.080
where some of the same
security people

00:51.115 --> 00:52.749
who let the terrorists
on the plane

00:52.783 --> 00:54.717
are still gainfully employed.

00:54.752 --> 00:57.553
With such low fares,
even those who never fly

00:57.588 --> 01:00.323
are lining up
to buy tickets.

01:00.357 --> 01:01.524
Early reports suggest

01:01.558 --> 01:03.593
that this could be
the most profitable day

01:03.627 --> 01:06.329
for airlines in
the last two decades.

01:06.363 --> 01:07.630
I'm Tricia Takanawa.

01:07.665 --> 01:08.765
Back to you, Tom.

01:08.799 --> 01:09.799
Hi, Mom.

01:09.833 --> 01:11.567
(in Joe's voice):
Hi, Joe.

01:11.602 --> 01:13.369
Wow, Peter,
did you hear that?

01:13.404 --> 01:14.404
Low airfares.

01:14.438 --> 01:15.805
Why don't we
take a trip?

01:15.839 --> 01:17.707
We never go
anywhere anymore.

01:17.741 --> 01:18.708
We're gettin' stagnant.

01:18.742 --> 01:19.709
What are you
talking about?

01:19.743 --> 01:20.710
We go places.

01:20.744 --> 01:22.845
We took that helicopter
tour of Tucson.

01:22.880 --> 01:24.814
Wow. I've never seen
so many boats

01:24.848 --> 01:26.683
not near bodies of water.

01:26.717 --> 01:28.818
That park is all concrete.

01:28.852 --> 01:31.721
Look, there's a dog
tied to a other dog.

01:31.755 --> 01:34.157
I think that guy with
long hair is a lawyer.

01:34.191 --> 01:37.627
Look at all those students
running for their lives.

01:37.661 --> 01:39.829
Oh, there's
the concrete park again.

01:39.863 --> 01:42.298
Peter, I'm talking
about real travel.

01:42.333 --> 01:44.467
We can finally afford
to go somewhere good.

01:44.501 --> 01:45.835
Joe and Bonnie
went to Europe

01:45.869 --> 01:47.437
and had the time
of their lives.

01:47.471 --> 01:49.739
Hey, I know a place
you can go, Lois: Hell.

01:49.773 --> 01:51.674
There, I said it.
Now shut your mouth.

01:51.709 --> 01:53.076
Lois, I hate travelling.

01:53.110 --> 01:55.111
Besides, my passport
picture is awful.

01:58.182 --> 02:00.383
Ugh, you look like
a human cigarette.

02:00.417 --> 02:02.118
But it's not just
about you, Peter.

02:02.152 --> 02:03.286
Think about the family.

02:03.320 --> 02:05.655
It would be an amazing
experience for the kids.

02:05.689 --> 02:07.390
It would give them
some culture.

02:07.424 --> 02:09.325
Lois, the kids can get
all the culture they need

02:09.360 --> 02:10.560
right here in Quahog.

02:10.594 --> 02:11.561
Really?

02:11.595 --> 02:12.829
More culture
than Europe?

02:12.863 --> 02:14.697
That's right, and I'm
gonna prove it to you.

02:14.732 --> 02:16.566
I got more culture
in my pinkie toe

02:16.600 --> 02:18.001
than all of Europe
put together.

02:20.104 --> 02:22.105
(singing opera)

02:30.180 --> 02:32.982
(wild applause)

02:33.017 --> 02:34.250
Oh, my God!

02:34.284 --> 02:37.420
I got ringworm, too,
but it's not classy like this.

02:39.957 --> 02:42.892
Okay, kids, now this is
the Quahog Museum of Science.

02:42.926 --> 02:46.496
A magical place
of cultural exploration.

02:46.530 --> 02:49.499
♪

02:57.107 --> 02:58.775
That sucked.

03:00.310 --> 03:02.345
Now, one thing that
makes us more cultured

03:02.379 --> 03:05.014
than our European counterparts
is high school football.

03:05.049 --> 03:07.250
Then why are we standing here
in the parking lot?

03:07.284 --> 03:08.951
Because sometimes
your team loses,

03:08.986 --> 03:10.453
and you have to
beat up the ref.

03:12.456 --> 03:14.023
What part
of Eatonville you from, huh?

03:14.058 --> 03:15.858
What part of Eatonville
you from?

03:15.893 --> 03:17.427
Ow! West Eatonville.

03:17.461 --> 03:18.461
Oh, really?
West Eatonville?

03:18.495 --> 03:19.762
Do you know Bob Hanley?

03:19.797 --> 03:21.597
I'm Bob Hanley.
No kidding!

03:21.632 --> 03:23.132
Bob, it's me, Peter Griffin.

03:23.167 --> 03:24.967
Oh, my God,
how the hell are you?

03:25.002 --> 03:26.803
I'm doing great.
I'm doing great.

03:26.837 --> 03:28.004
Well, hey, oh, listen.

03:28.038 --> 03:29.205
I was really sorry
to hear about your dad.

03:29.239 --> 03:30.640
Oh, that means a lot.

03:30.674 --> 03:32.708
Yeah, I'm sorry
I couldn't make it to the wake.

03:32.743 --> 03:33.876
I was out of town.

03:33.911 --> 03:35.344
It's okay.
Thanks for the flowers.

03:35.379 --> 03:36.779
Oh, it's the least I could do.

03:36.814 --> 03:37.914
Hey, Chris, get over here.

03:37.948 --> 03:40.216
You got to meet
my buddy Bob Hanley.

03:40.250 --> 03:42.452
Hi, Mr. Hanley.
I'm Chris.

03:42.486 --> 03:45.555
I heard a lot about you, sir.

03:48.659 --> 03:51.394
<i>We now return
to Downton Syndrome.</i>

03:51.428 --> 03:53.729
Well, if I must accept
your proposal in order

03:53.764 --> 03:56.199
to break the entail
and save my family's estate,

03:56.233 --> 03:58.668
then yes, I will marry you.

03:58.702 --> 04:00.470
Yay!

04:00.504 --> 04:03.005
Everyone,
I've got a surprise.

04:03.040 --> 04:05.808
Whoa!
I did not see that coming.

04:05.843 --> 04:07.677
No, Chris,
I haven't said it yet.

04:07.711 --> 04:09.846
Today was the last day
of the low airfares,

04:09.880 --> 04:13.082
and I couldn't let
the opportunity pass, so...

04:13.117 --> 04:15.418
I got us tickets to Italy!

04:15.452 --> 04:16.853
That's amazing, Mom!

04:16.887 --> 04:18.287
Lois, is Peter
okay with this?

04:18.322 --> 04:21.357
Well, Brian, I haven't
quite told him yet.

04:21.391 --> 04:22.658
But what if he says no?

04:22.693 --> 04:24.227
He can't say no.

04:24.261 --> 04:25.695
I already
bought the tickets.

04:25.729 --> 04:26.696
Tickets for what?

04:26.730 --> 04:27.763
Uh, uh...

04:27.798 --> 04:30.299
tickets for the, um,
the grand opening

04:30.334 --> 04:33.469
of the biggest water park
in all of Rhode Island.

04:33.504 --> 04:35.338
Hmm, well, pardon
my skepticism, Lois,

04:35.372 --> 04:37.406
but I didn't read
about this in the latest issue

04:37.441 --> 04:39.509
<i>of Water Park Enthusiast Weekly.</i>

04:39.543 --> 04:42.879
Well, they-they had
to, um, keep it secret.

04:42.913 --> 04:45.414
For fear that, um...

04:45.449 --> 04:46.916
th-that this new park would put

04:46.950 --> 04:49.152
all other water parks
out of business.

04:49.186 --> 04:50.453
Typical Big Water.

04:50.487 --> 04:52.155
Always keeping the
public in the dark.

04:52.189 --> 04:54.390
So this one's bigger
than Raging Waters?

04:54.424 --> 04:56.125
Yeah, Dad.
It's way bigger.

04:56.160 --> 04:57.326
How does the boy know?

04:57.361 --> 04:58.661
Uh, because I-I told him.

04:58.695 --> 05:00.863
I-I know someone who-who helped,
uh, build it.

05:00.898 --> 05:03.199
Wow, so we really got an
inside track on this thing.

05:03.233 --> 05:05.234
But it can't be bigger
than Six Flags Atlantis.

05:05.269 --> 05:07.336
Oh, it is, Peter, it is.

05:07.371 --> 05:08.538
Because...

05:08.572 --> 05:11.407
it's Seven Flags Atlantis.

05:11.441 --> 05:14.010
(gasps) That's a whole flag
more of water!

05:14.044 --> 05:15.211
Get in the car!

05:15.245 --> 05:17.380
Okay, everyone,
put the sunblock on now,

05:17.414 --> 05:19.415
so when we get off the plane,
we go right to the park.

05:19.449 --> 05:21.150
Wait, why are we on a plane?

05:21.185 --> 05:23.586
Uh, you-you want to beat the
lines, don't you, Peter?

05:23.620 --> 05:24.887
All-all right, Brian, enough.

05:24.922 --> 05:27.056
I-I can't
do this anymore.

05:27.090 --> 05:29.425
Peter, we're not
going to a water park.

05:29.459 --> 05:31.060
We're going to Italy.

05:31.094 --> 05:33.629
What?! But what about
Seven Flags Atlantis?

05:33.664 --> 05:35.031
Peter, don't be silly.

05:35.065 --> 05:36.899
Th-There's no such place.

05:36.934 --> 05:38.267
(over P.A.):
This is your captain speaking.

05:38.302 --> 05:40.369
If you look out the left side
of the airplane,

05:40.404 --> 05:43.105
you'll see the very popular
Seven Flags Atlantis.

05:43.140 --> 05:44.941
Aw!

05:44.975 --> 05:48.377
(children laughing)

05:48.412 --> 05:50.379
I'm gonna go ahead
and turn off the seatbelt sign,

05:50.414 --> 05:54.183
so you idiots can all stretch
and let the fart festival begin.

05:54.218 --> 05:55.885
Why are you such
a dick, Jim?

05:55.919 --> 05:58.354
I'm just mad
about pilot stuff.

06:03.880 --> 06:06.081
I can't believe you tricked
me into going to Italy.

06:06.116 --> 06:08.417
Peter, our family could use
a trip like this.

06:08.451 --> 06:11.053
Plus, it might be good
for you and me, too.

06:11.087 --> 06:14.189
It's the most romantic country
in the whole world.

06:14.224 --> 06:16.692
And God knows we could use a
little romance in our marriage.

06:16.726 --> 06:17.960
What are you talking about?

06:17.994 --> 06:20.062
Just the other night
we did that role-playing.

06:20.096 --> 06:23.499
Did you like that game of
mysterious intruder, Lois?

06:23.533 --> 06:25.301
I told you--
I don't know who you are.

06:25.335 --> 06:27.102
And my name isn't Lois.

06:27.137 --> 06:28.504
Mine is.

06:28.538 --> 06:30.439
Let's do it again.

06:37.814 --> 06:39.548
Is it true there's
no drinking age here?

06:39.582 --> 06:41.317
And they serve wine
at every meal?

06:41.351 --> 06:44.053
Oh, Meg, nobody
likes a drunky-chunky.

06:44.087 --> 06:47.356
In college, a lot of guys
are gonna make you a dare.

06:48.792 --> 06:50.926
Hey, you know what, guys?
We're six hours ahead here.

06:50.960 --> 06:52.761
I'm gonna call Joe
and tell him the future.

06:52.796 --> 06:54.396
(buttons beeping)

06:54.431 --> 06:56.665
Hey, Joe, guess
what's about to happen?

06:56.700 --> 06:57.733
Who is this?

06:57.767 --> 06:58.734
It's Peter.

06:58.768 --> 06:59.768
That's impossible.

06:59.803 --> 07:01.603
Peter died six hours ago.

07:01.638 --> 07:02.638
(gasps)

07:02.672 --> 07:03.772
We can't go back.

07:13.283 --> 07:15.150
Look, kids,
this is the Forum,

07:15.185 --> 07:16.685
where the ancient Romans
used to have

07:16.720 --> 07:18.487
their famous
political debates.

07:18.521 --> 07:21.223
<i>And this is the Penthouse Forum,
where dirtbags write letters</i>

07:21.257 --> 07:23.325
about boning in the
back of grocery stores.

07:23.360 --> 07:25.440
Well, who wants to see where
the ancients established

07:25.462 --> 07:27.896
the foundation of
our American democracy?

07:27.931 --> 07:30.132
And who wants to hear
a story about a girl

07:30.166 --> 07:31.926
who moved to New York
without any underpants?

07:33.269 --> 07:34.570
I don't know
why we even go places.

07:34.604 --> 07:36.805
We always just end up
reading pornography.

07:40.176 --> 07:42.978
<i>We now return
to Italian Star Trek.</i>

07:43.012 --> 07:45.180
I know I supposed
to be-a so logical,

07:45.215 --> 07:48.283
but I get-a so steaming a-mad!

07:48.318 --> 07:51.520
Peter, the kids are going
to the Italian circus,

07:51.554 --> 07:53.889
so I thought
it might be romantic

07:53.923 --> 07:56.191
if we watched the sunset
from St. Peter's Basilica.

07:56.226 --> 07:58.266
You go ahead, I'm gonna stay
here and get frustrated

07:58.294 --> 08:01.230
with this nonresponsive
hotel TV remote.

08:01.264 --> 08:03.499
Come on! Guide!

08:03.533 --> 08:05.534
I'm pressing "Guide"!

08:07.704 --> 08:10.139
Stupid remote's
not doing anything!

08:14.544 --> 08:16.311
Stop what you're doing!

08:16.346 --> 08:18.280
Peter, you can't just
sit in front of the TV

08:18.314 --> 08:19.615
the entire vacation.

08:19.649 --> 08:21.183
You know, I'm really getting
tired of you being

08:21.217 --> 08:22.951
such a complete
stick-in-the-mud.

08:22.986 --> 08:24.553
Is that some kind
of gay slur?

08:24.587 --> 08:26.188
I can't believe it's
too much for you

08:26.222 --> 08:27.623
to watch a sunset
with your wife

08:27.657 --> 08:29.691
in one of the most romantic
cities in the world!

08:29.726 --> 08:30.826
But, you know what?

08:30.860 --> 08:32.060
It's probably for the best.

08:32.095 --> 08:33.896
Because you'd just
complain about it!

08:33.930 --> 08:35.531
Well, what do you expect, Lois?
I didn't want to come here

08:35.565 --> 08:37.800
he first place.
I'd rather be back home.

08:37.834 --> 08:40.402
You know, I could be
riding seesaw with Joe.

08:40.437 --> 08:42.438
Yay!

08:44.607 --> 08:45.841
Well, I guess we're stuck.

08:45.875 --> 08:48.210
Hang on.
Let me empty this bag.

08:48.244 --> 08:50.479
Yay, gross.

08:50.513 --> 08:52.414
Fine, you just sit
here on the couch.

08:52.449 --> 08:55.551
I'll go enjoy
Italy without you!

08:57.420 --> 08:59.121
God, what the hell's
her problem?

08:59.155 --> 09:00.556
You know, sometimes
I think you forget

09:00.590 --> 09:01.957
how lucky you
are to have Lois.

09:01.991 --> 09:04.560
Otherwise, you wouldn't be
acting like such a jerk.

09:04.594 --> 09:06.128
Oh, I'm getting it
from you now?

09:06.162 --> 09:07.296
Don't worry about Lois.

09:07.330 --> 09:08.931
She's an American woman
in Italy.

09:08.965 --> 09:10.499
She'll go out,
kill her roommate

09:10.533 --> 09:12.468
during a sex game,
and then she'll be fine.

09:12.502 --> 09:14.169
She might come back
even hotter.

09:14.204 --> 09:15.904
Well, you might want
to look out there.

09:18.641 --> 09:21.076
What the hell's she doing
with that Italian guy?

09:21.110 --> 09:22.611
(door opens, closes)

09:22.645 --> 09:26.014
CHRIS: Hey, guys? Do I got to tip
the gondolier in here?

09:26.049 --> 09:28.750
I, uh, got a little blockage.

09:28.785 --> 09:32.654
♪ I break-a the poops up

09:32.689 --> 09:36.558
♪ In-a little chunks...

09:36.593 --> 09:39.761
I wish I had someone special
to share this with.

09:39.796 --> 09:42.064
Let me give you a tour
on-a the Vespa.

09:42.098 --> 09:44.233
I show you all-a the things
I do all day

09:44.267 --> 09:46.201
that make-a me smell like this.

09:46.236 --> 09:49.838
Hey, Lois, what the hell's
going on here?

09:49.873 --> 09:51.874
Something you know
nothing about.

09:53.743 --> 09:55.377
Oh, man, she really is upset.

09:55.411 --> 09:58.080
MAN: Having lady
problems, are you, pal?

09:58.114 --> 09:59.181
Dean Martin?!

09:59.215 --> 10:00.282
What are you doing here?

10:00.316 --> 10:02.317
<i>I thought you were dead.
Well, paisan,</i>

10:02.352 --> 10:05.687
as long as love is
alive in Italy, so am I.

10:05.722 --> 10:08.123
Now, look, buddy, there's
a million ways to lose a gal,

10:08.157 --> 10:10.626
but there's only one sure way
to get her back.

10:10.660 --> 10:13.128
Turn on the old Italian charm.

10:13.162 --> 10:14.396
But I'm not Italian.

10:14.430 --> 10:16.031
Well, you're in Italy,
ain't you?

10:16.065 --> 10:18.467
I think so, I-I haven't
been paying attention.

10:18.501 --> 10:19.835
Now, if you'll excuse me,

10:19.869 --> 10:23.805
I'm gonna ride this rigatoni
into the sky.

10:23.840 --> 10:26.008
<i>♪ Volare ♪</i>

10:26.042 --> 10:28.477
♪ Oh, oh, oh, oh

10:30.113 --> 10:33.048
<i>♪ Cantare ♪</i>

10:33.082 --> 10:36.451
♪ Oh, ho-ho-ho-ho...

10:36.486 --> 10:40.088
♪ Let's fly way up
to the clouds... ♪

10:40.123 --> 10:41.757
(low whimpering)

10:41.791 --> 10:43.091
Dean, wait!

10:45.995 --> 10:47.296
BRIAN:
Hey! Get away from him!

10:47.330 --> 10:48.764
Peter, you okay?

10:48.798 --> 10:51.133
Brian? What happened?

10:51.167 --> 10:52.534
I was watching you
from the window.

10:52.569 --> 10:54.102
When Lois pulled away
on that motorcycle,

10:54.137 --> 10:56.204
you laid down on the ground
and went to sleep.

10:56.239 --> 10:57.873
Well, I'm awake
now, Brian.

10:57.907 --> 10:59.775
And I know what
I've got to do.

10:59.809 --> 11:00.943
Well, before you
do anything,

11:00.977 --> 11:02.444
for God's sake,
put some clothes on.

11:02.478 --> 11:03.579
Brian, calm down.

11:03.613 --> 11:05.847
In some countries,
this is a compliment.

11:06.849 --> 11:08.116
Mmm.

11:08.151 --> 11:09.184
Mmmm!

11:09.218 --> 11:11.920
Oh, my goodness! Mmm-mm!

11:11.955 --> 11:13.822
Oh, please, come on.

11:13.856 --> 11:16.892
Is just something
I threw together.

11:20.630 --> 11:21.863
What the hell?

11:21.898 --> 11:23.665
<i>Mi amore!</i>

11:23.700 --> 11:27.069
I am here to put you on a
pedestal with Italian romance.

11:28.771 --> 11:30.806
Look, Peter, showing up here
in that stupid costume

11:30.840 --> 11:32.341
isn't going to win me over.

11:32.375 --> 11:35.277
That's because you lack
the passion of true Italian.

11:35.311 --> 11:37.412
<i>Mi amore!</i>

11:37.447 --> 11:39.214
Peter, stop it.

11:39.248 --> 11:41.149
Speak with your heart,
not your mouth.

11:41.184 --> 11:43.285
Ugh, damn it, Peter!

11:43.319 --> 11:45.020
You mean "Pietro."

11:46.422 --> 11:47.756
Well, by the laws
of Italy,

11:47.790 --> 11:50.392
I must now kiss
myself passionately.

11:53.062 --> 11:54.563
Peter, are you
all right?

11:54.597 --> 11:56.098
Are you kidding?
I'm great!

11:56.132 --> 11:57.633
♪ Bouncing around Italia

11:57.667 --> 11:59.534
♪ 'Cause I'm a little ball

11:59.569 --> 12:03.005
♪ I can fit wherever,
'cause I am really small ♪

12:03.039 --> 12:06.241
And now, the next scene.

12:06.275 --> 12:08.744
(crowd cheering)

12:08.778 --> 12:10.746
Peter, put me down!

12:10.780 --> 12:12.047
Lois, to prove my love,

12:12.081 --> 12:14.416
I have convinced
20,000 soccer fans

12:14.450 --> 12:17.686
to hold up a romantic
message just for you.

12:20.890 --> 12:21.857
Peter!

12:21.891 --> 12:22.891
Oh, they missed a letter.

12:31.567 --> 12:34.770
Okay, I'll admit it--
this is pretty romantic.

12:34.804 --> 12:36.371
Listen, Lois.

12:36.406 --> 12:38.607
I'm sorry for being
such a jerk, all right?

12:38.641 --> 12:40.375
I love you.

12:42.412 --> 12:44.846
I want to have your baby.

12:44.881 --> 12:47.516
Peter, not on a boat.

12:47.550 --> 12:48.884
Peter...

12:48.918 --> 12:50.585
Oh, Peter...

12:50.620 --> 12:52.421
Come on, Lois, I got
a ninety-percenter going.

12:52.422 --> 12:54.756
It could go either way.
Let's start making choices.

12:54.791 --> 12:56.692
Oh, Peter...

12:56.726 --> 12:58.660
I love you.

12:58.695 --> 13:00.996
I love you, too.

13:09.872 --> 13:12.874
Wow, Peter, last night was
the best sex I've ever had.

13:12.909 --> 13:14.843
Me, too.

13:14.877 --> 13:16.678
We haven't done it like that
since we were engaged

13:16.713 --> 13:19.848
but allowed to sleep
with other people.

13:19.882 --> 13:22.517
The point is, Italy is the best
thing that ever happened to us.

13:22.552 --> 13:24.586
I know, it's been
wonderful.

13:24.620 --> 13:26.321
I wish we never
had to leave.

13:26.355 --> 13:28.790
Well, then, let's not.
Let's live here.

13:28.825 --> 13:30.459
What? Peter, but...

13:30.493 --> 13:32.928
but we've built a life
back in Quahog.

13:32.962 --> 13:34.296
Yeah, but you
were right, Lois,

13:34.330 --> 13:35.997
we were stagnating
back home.

13:36.032 --> 13:38.333
Let's start a new life,
right here in Italy.

13:41.838 --> 13:43.638
Peter, those were our passports!

13:43.673 --> 13:44.806
We don't need them anymore.

13:44.841 --> 13:46.942
Kids, I got an announcement.

13:46.976 --> 13:48.376
He's wearing
a rubber.

13:48.411 --> 13:50.812
We're staying in Italy.
We're Italian now.

13:50.847 --> 13:52.147
What?!
Are you kidding?!

13:52.181 --> 13:53.482
Well, if we're
going to be Italian,

13:53.516 --> 13:55.717
I guess we should start
murdering our brothers.

13:57.120 --> 13:58.553
What are you...?
(gagging)

13:58.588 --> 14:00.388
You break-a
my heart, Chris.

14:00.423 --> 14:01.857
You break-a my heart.

14:09.423 --> 14:11.658
("Funiculi Funicula" plays)

14:11.692 --> 14:12.725
(bicycle bell dings)

14:12.760 --> 14:14.961
Hey, Peter,
what's-a you rush?

14:14.995 --> 14:17.564
I got-a the most beautiful woman
in the world at home,

14:17.598 --> 14:19.399
and I no wanna keep her waiting.

14:19.433 --> 14:22.068
Hey, Vincenzo,
play me a tune, huh?

14:22.103 --> 14:24.471
(playing "Yankee Doodle Dandy")

14:24.505 --> 14:27.740
<i>Oh, Vincenzo!
Insalata Caprese!</i>

14:27.775 --> 14:30.777
("Funiculi Funicula" resumes)

14:30.811 --> 14:32.545
Ah, Italy.

14:32.580 --> 14:33.913
It 9:00 a.m.

14:33.948 --> 14:36.015
and I've already kissed
eight guys twice.

14:36.050 --> 14:37.951
Why-a you looking
at other babies, huh?

14:37.985 --> 14:40.053
Why you make-a me
do that, Ruperto?

14:40.087 --> 14:41.221
I no wanna do that!

14:41.255 --> 14:42.555
Peter, I know you
love it here,

14:42.590 --> 14:43.990
but riding your
bike around town

14:44.024 --> 14:45.158
isn't paying the bills,

14:45.192 --> 14:46.860
and the kids aren't
learning anything

14:46.894 --> 14:48.628
at that Italian school.

14:48.662 --> 14:50.463
Look at these
big ones, huh?

14:50.498 --> 14:51.798
Rut-ta-ta ta-ta,

14:51.832 --> 14:54.534
rut-ta-ta ta-ta,
rut-ta-ta ta-ta!

14:54.568 --> 14:55.802
Is this calculus?

14:55.836 --> 14:56.970
I don't know.

14:57.004 --> 14:59.139
Rut-ta-ta ta-ta!

14:59.173 --> 15:00.406
I think maybe it's time

15:00.441 --> 15:02.642
to go back to our
real home in Quahog.

15:02.676 --> 15:04.244
But I thought
you loved Italy.

15:04.278 --> 15:05.645
And Italian Peter.

15:05.679 --> 15:07.247
And Southern
Italian Peter.

15:07.281 --> 15:08.882
Olive oil!

15:08.916 --> 15:11.818
I do, but I love
American Peter even more.

15:11.852 --> 15:12.986
Lois is right.

15:13.020 --> 15:14.354
We're barely
surviving here.

15:14.388 --> 15:15.889
I mean, you have to give
most of what you earn

15:15.923 --> 15:18.625
to the mafia that
terrorizes this village.

15:18.659 --> 15:20.660
(tires screeching)

15:54.528 --> 15:57.163
Peter, I think it's time
we all go back home.

15:57.198 --> 15:58.598
(sighs):
Of course we have to leave

15:58.632 --> 16:00.700
as soon as I get
an Italian boyfriend.

16:00.734 --> 16:01.768
You know, I still
don't understand

16:01.802 --> 16:03.069
what he does for a living.

16:03.103 --> 16:05.438
He gathers coins from the air
by jumping on mushrooms.

16:05.472 --> 16:07.240
What's so hard to understand?!

16:07.274 --> 16:08.608
("Super Mario Bros."
music plays)

16:08.642 --> 16:10.343
Hey, Meg, let's go-a
two-player!

16:10.377 --> 16:12.011
I got-a Luigi
here, huh?

16:12.046 --> 16:13.079
Hey, Meg-a!

16:13.113 --> 16:16.249
Mario said-a you were
into-a weird-a stuff.

16:19.653 --> 16:21.087
So, you see, Mr. Consulate,

16:21.121 --> 16:23.289
all we want to do is get back
to our home in America.

16:23.324 --> 16:25.458
So, can we have
our new passports now?

16:25.492 --> 16:27.360
Mr. Griffin, it's not
as simple as that.

16:27.394 --> 16:29.462
Your family must take
a citizenship test

16:29.496 --> 16:30.997
and go through
the immigration process

16:31.031 --> 16:32.665
like any other
foreigner.

16:32.700 --> 16:34.767
Foreigner?
We're not foreigners.

16:34.802 --> 16:35.802
Sorry, Mrs. Griffin.

16:35.836 --> 16:37.570
Not only did you
destroy your passports,

16:37.605 --> 16:39.505
you renounced our citizenship.

16:39.540 --> 16:40.640
When did we do that?

16:40.674 --> 16:41.708
Oh, I did that

16:41.742 --> 16:43.176
on the Italian social
networking site,

16:43.210 --> 16:44.811
"Shut-up-a-you-facebook."

16:54.955 --> 16:56.889
All right, Meg, part of
being an American citizen

16:56.924 --> 17:00.159
is having an opinion on certain
hot-button political issues.

17:00.194 --> 17:02.528
Now, what is your
abortion stance?

17:02.563 --> 17:05.164
It's pretty much this.

17:06.700 --> 17:08.501
Now, if you will,
please sing

17:08.535 --> 17:10.937
the national anthem
of the United States.

17:10.971 --> 17:14.140
♪ O say can you see

17:14.174 --> 17:18.344
♪ By the dawn's early light

17:18.379 --> 17:21.614
♪ What so proudly we hailed

17:21.649 --> 17:25.118
♪ At the twilight's
last gleaming ♪

17:25.152 --> 17:28.121
♪ And I...

17:28.155 --> 17:29.255
♪ Eee-I...

17:29.290 --> 17:30.256
(inhales deeply)

17:30.291 --> 17:34.861
♪ Will always love you-ooh...

17:34.895 --> 17:37.530
♪ Oh-I-ee-I...

17:37.564 --> 17:39.499
Okay, I've reviewed
your application.

17:39.533 --> 17:40.867
Now, where
you listed employment,

17:40.901 --> 17:42.502
some of you wrote "Family Guy,"

17:42.536 --> 17:44.370
and others wrote
"The Family Guy."

17:44.405 --> 17:45.471
Which one is it?

17:46.740 --> 17:48.675
(sighs):
It's "Family Guy."

17:48.709 --> 17:49.942
All right,
now that you've completed

17:49.977 --> 17:51.110
your initial application,

17:51.145 --> 17:53.065
it will need to be filed
by an immigration lawyer

17:53.080 --> 17:56.082
at a cost of approximately
$5,000 per person.

17:56.116 --> 17:57.817
And assuming
that all goes through,

17:57.851 --> 18:00.987
you should have your
citizenship back in 16 months.

18:01.021 --> 18:02.322
Sixteen months?

18:02.356 --> 18:03.790
If I didn't know any better,

18:03.824 --> 18:05.058
I'd think you were making it

18:05.092 --> 18:06.859
hard to become
a citizen on purpose.

18:06.894 --> 18:09.662
Like you don't want any
immigrants coming to the U.S.

18:09.697 --> 18:11.097
I wish that were true,
Mrs. Griffin,

18:11.098 --> 18:12.699
and it probably might be.

18:12.733 --> 18:15.535
Now, if you'll excuse me,
I have to go sit on my fat wife.

18:15.569 --> 18:18.738
WOMAN: I'm ready for you,
String Bean!

18:18.772 --> 18:20.506
What a weird guy.

18:20.541 --> 18:21.908
Oh, this is terrible.

18:21.942 --> 18:23.676
Peter, what are
we going to do?

18:23.711 --> 18:25.211
I'll tell you what
we're going to do.

18:25.245 --> 18:26.479
We're going
to sneak in.

18:26.513 --> 18:28.181
Well, we better get there soon.

18:28.215 --> 18:31.417
<i>I want to be there for opening
night of Karate Kid 5.</i>

18:31.452 --> 18:33.252
Mr. Miyagi, this is
kind of embarrassing,

18:33.287 --> 18:36.222
but I'm having a problem
with erectile dysfunction.

18:36.256 --> 18:38.424
Aye, Daniel-san.
Miyagi fix.

18:47.501 --> 18:48.534
(horn honks)

19:12.092 --> 19:14.093
(crane whirring)

19:44.391 --> 19:47.760
(clearing throat)

19:50.864 --> 19:54.000
Well, I'm so glad to finally
be back home in Quahog.

19:54.034 --> 19:55.435
Although,
I will miss Italy.

19:55.469 --> 19:59.071
You made the trip very
special for me, Peter.

19:59.106 --> 20:01.007
Well, thanks for showing
me and the kids

20:01.041 --> 20:02.775
what the rest of
the world is like, Lois.

20:02.810 --> 20:04.710
I think we all learned a lot.

20:04.745 --> 20:06.245
And we met a lot
of great people.

20:06.280 --> 20:08.448
Hey, what about that
couple we met in Amalfi?

20:08.482 --> 20:09.482
Did you get their number?

20:09.516 --> 20:10.516
I did. You ready?
Yup.

20:10.551 --> 20:14.620
Okay, it's
19-565-55018...

20:14.655 --> 20:16.889
81-453...

20:16.924 --> 20:18.591
989-90...

20:18.625 --> 20:22.628
016-8945-2.

20:22.663 --> 20:25.031
Well, I can't wait to see the
rest of the world with you guys.

20:25.065 --> 20:26.499
(pounding at door)

20:27.901 --> 20:29.635
Mario! You came for me!

20:29.670 --> 20:31.537
Come with me, Meg,
my princess.

20:31.572 --> 20:34.740
We gonna take this relationship
to the next level.

20:34.775 --> 20:36.776
(game music playing)

20:41.344 --> 20:42.644
MEG:
Luigi's here again?

20:43.097 --> 20:44.398
I thought that was
a one-time thing.

20:44.432 --> 20:46.517
MARIO:
He like-a my sloppy seconds.

20:46.518 --> 20:47.485
LUIGI:
We-a plumber brothers.

20:47.519 --> 20:49.487
We-a not-a high class people.

20:49.488 --> 21:50.488
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru